-- The diagrams in these three articles are fenced ```d2 sketch```. The first
-- seed dropped the bare `sketch` flag and rendered them unsketched; this sets
-- each body to the rebuilt seed's, whose diagrams point at the sketch SVGs
-- (new content-hashed keys on R2). A no-op after a seed built from the fixed
-- scripts/build-seeds.ts. Apply to platform-articles.
UPDATE `articles` SET `body` = '{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Welcome to the Rawkode Academy! We''re thrilled to start talking about our new platform, designed to provide developers with an immersive and engaging learning experience."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"In this article, we''ll take you behind the scenes, exploring the innovative patterns and techniques we''ve employed to build a robust, scalable, and cloud-native platform. From CQRS and GraphQL Federation to Restate and our unique approach to service-oriented architecture, we''ll delve into the key components that power our platform."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"It''s my job to make content that helps developers learn stuff. That stuff has varied over the last ten years and I assume that will continue to be the case. Which appeals a lot for me, because I like shiny things."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"So when I decided to remove my dependency on YouTube and build my own platform, I decided to have some fun."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Let me introduce you to the different patterns and techniques we''ve been using to build out the platform."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"This article will be a little high level, but stay tuned! Over the next few weeks we''ll be going deeper into each pattern and technique, with code samples."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Too impatient? Check out the "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://github.com/RawkodeAcademy/RawkodeAcademy","target":null,"rel":null,"class":null,"title":null}}],"text":"repository"},{"type":"text","text":" today."}]},{"type":"horizontalRule"},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"id":"cloud-native"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Cloud Native"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Given that I founded the Rawkode Academy to help make Cloud Native, a vast and ever changing landscape, easier for developers; it''s only fitting that whatever I build for the Rawkode Academy is built as Cloud Native as it can be."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"As such, the Rawkode Academy platform is built as a set of micro-services. Some are less micro and some are nano, so let''s just settle on that this is a service-oriented architecture with it sometimes taking some liberties and sometimes going to extremes."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"We also adopted a hybrid-cloud approach, leveraging bare metal, managed Kubernetes, and serverless."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Depending on what part of the platform you interact with; you''ll be hitting either:"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Website:"},{"type":"text","text":" Cloudflare Pages + Workers"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"GraphQL API:"},{"type":"text","text":" Cloud Run"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Video Encoding:"},{"type":"text","text":" Bare Metal on Equinix Metal"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Auth:"},{"type":"text","text":" GKE AutoPilot"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"We use GKE AutoPilot (with Spot) for consistently running services, such as "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://zitadel.com","target":null,"rel":null,"class":null,"title":null}}],"text":"Zitadel"},{"type":"text","text":"."}]}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I''ve taken a few liberties with the arrows on the diagram below (on the write path) in-order to have the diagram be more readable."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"All write requests, and some read (such as people) go through Zitadel for AuthN."}]},{"type":"diagram","attrs":{"id":"academy-architecture","title":"Academy read and write services","description":"The website queries the Cosmo GraphQL gateway, which routes to five read services and their databases. Each database also connects to a corresponding write service behind the Restate RPC gateway.","language":"d2","source":"# System architecture in D2\nWebsite: Website\n\nCosmo: Cosmo GraphQL Gateway\n\nWebsite -> Cosmo\n\n# GraphQL Read Services\nPeopleGraphQL: People Read\nShowsGraphQL: Shows Read\nShowHostsGraphQL: Show Hosts Read\nVideosGraphQL: Video Read\nTechnologiesGraphQL: Technologies Read\n\nCosmo -> PeopleGraphQL\nCosmo -> ShowsGraphQL\nCosmo -> ShowHostsGraphQL\nCosmo -> VideosGraphQL\nCosmo -> TechnologiesGraphQL\n\n# Databases\nPeopleDB: People Database\nShowsDB: Shows Database\nShowHostsDB: ShowHosts Database\nVideosDB: Videos Database\nTechnologiesDB: Technologies Database\n\nPeopleGraphQL -> PeopleDB\nShowsGraphQL -> ShowsDB\nShowHostsGraphQL -> ShowHostsDB\nVideosGraphQL -> VideosDB\nTechnologiesGraphQL -> TechnologiesDB\n\n# Restate RPC Gateway\nRestate: Restate RPC Gateway\n\n# Restate Write Services\nPeopleRestate: People Write\nShowsRestate: Shows Write\nShowHostsRestate: Show Hosts Write\nVideosRestate: Video Write\nTechnologiesRestate: Technologies Write\n\nPeopleRestate <-> Restate\nShowsRestate <-> Restate\nShowHostsRestate <-> Restate\nVideosRestate <-> Restate\nTechnologiesRestate <-> Restate\n\nPeopleDB <-> PeopleRestate\nShowsDB <-> ShowsRestate\nShowHostsDB <-> ShowHostsRestate\nVideosDB <-> VideosRestate\nTechnologiesDB <-> TechnologiesRestate\n\n# Optional styling (uncomment and modify as needed)\n# direction: right\n# Website.style.fill: lightblue\n# Cosmo.style.fill: lightgreen\n# Restate.style.fill: lightyellow","meta":"sketch","src":"https://content.rawkode.academy/articles/our-architecture/diagrams/4c1747ebe1c00e58.svg"}},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"id":"command-query-responsibility-segregation-cqrs"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Command Query Responsibility Segregation (CQRS)"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I''ve been a huge fan of the work of "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://udidahan.com/","target":null,"rel":null,"class":null,"title":null}}],"text":"Udi Dahan"},{"type":"text","text":" and Greg Young for over ten years, even implementing my own CQRS & Event Sourcing libraries in a variety of languages (I actually used this as my project to learn new languages)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Event Sourcing wasn''t required for the platform, but CQRS is a fantastic pattern to implement in almost any project. It allows you to separate the read and write models for your application."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"In a strict CQRS fashion, these would likely actually have different views / data stores / projections; but we don''t need to adopt this quite yet."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Our read API is powered by GraphQL, with federation, and our write API is powered by "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://restate.dev","target":null,"rel":null,"class":null,"title":null}}],"text":"Restate"},{"type":"text","text":"."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"id":"graphql-federation"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"GraphQL Federation"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"We use a single GraphQL Gateway, powered by "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://cosmo.wundergraph.com","target":null,"rel":null,"class":null,"title":null}}],"text":"WunderGraph Cosmo"},{"type":"text","text":", which can build a query plan for your GraphQL query. This query plan is realised by individual GraphQL APIs, to the object or field level, provided by each service."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"This allows us to have a single endpoint for all our read operations, while still allowing each service to own its data and schema. This is a fantastic pattern for a service-oriented architecture. The icing on the cake for us with this pattern is that with GraphQL Federation we can actually extend the read model and its schemata with new fields, joins, and pretty much anything we need; all without dealing with database migrations."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"We''ll be diving into this in great detail on our next article."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"id":"restate"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Restate"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"We use Restate as a HTTP RPC layer with built-in queues and durable execution which provides commands to write data to our, currently, shared data stores."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Currently, each service shares a data store for both reads and writes. We prioritize write optimization through our RPC commands, as read optimization is not yet a concern."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"We''ll dive into our RPC and Restate in a future article, but here''s a quick example of how we use it."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"We used Restate, with its durable execution, as writing to our platform is a complex task."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Due to the service-oriented architecture, scheduling a new live stream is a multi-part process; but we want a simple command to do so."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Imagine the following write:"}]},{"type":"codeBlock","attrs":{"language":"shell","title":null,"meta":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"curl -XPOST https://rpc.rawkode.academy/live-stream/new -d ''{\n  \"title\": \"Overview of the Rawkode Academy Architecture\",\n  \"hosts\": [ \"rawkode\" ],\n  \"guests\": [ \"icepuma\" ],\n  \"when\": \"2024-12-23Z10:00:00:Z\",\n  \"description\": \"BLAH\",\n  \"technologies\": [\"Restate\", \"Rust\", \"TypeScript\", \"GraphQL\", \"CQRS\"]\n}''"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"This is not a complete example, but to understand the complexity we need to understand this such a request is a "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://microservices.io/patterns/data/saga.html","target":null,"rel":null,"class":null,"title":null}}],"text":"SAGA"},{"type":"text","text":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"We need to write multiple entities to multiple services while also ensuring referential integrity across these boundaries."}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Can we schedule a live stream for a date in the past? "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"No"},{"type":"text","text":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Can we schedule an episode with host "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"rawkode"},{"type":"text","text":" if that identifier doesn''t exist in the "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"people"},{"type":"text","text":" service: "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"No"},{"type":"text","text":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Can we schedule an episode with guest "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"icepuma"},{"type":"text","text":" if that identifier doesn''t exist in the "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"people"},{"type":"text","text":" service: "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"No"},{"type":"text","text":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Same for technologies"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"and so forth"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"We aim to prevent write failures by implementing checks and balances. While eventual consistency is expected in a distributed system, these measures protect the integrity of the read model."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Again, stay tuned; there''s lots of examples and code we can share to dive into this deeper."}]},{"type":"horizontalRule"},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"In this article, we''ve explored the architecture and design choices behind the Rawkode Academy''s new platform. We highlighted our commitment to a cloud-native, service-oriented approach, leveraging technologies like Cloudflare Pages, Cloud Run, Equinix Metal, and GKE AutoPilot. We also shallow dived into our implementation of CQRS, GraphQL Federation, and Restate, explaining how these patterns contribute to a robust and scalable platform."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Our use of Restate for durable execution and handling complex write operations, particularly in the context of SAGAs, ensures data integrity and consistency across our distributed services. We''ve also touched on the importance of eventual consistency in such an environment."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"This high-level overview provides a glimpse into the underlying technologies and principles driving the Rawkode Academy platform."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Stay tuned for upcoming articles where we''ll deep dive into each component with detailed explanations and code samples."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Don''t want to miss the new articles?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Follow our "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://bsky.app/profile/rawkode.academy","target":null,"rel":null,"class":null,"title":null}}],"text":"BlueSky"},{"type":"text","text":" account to keep up to date with the platform build out and our new articles."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Until next time!"}]}]}' WHERE `id` = 'our-architecture';
UPDATE `articles` SET `body` = '{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"In this article, we''ll explore the design and implementation of our federated GraphQL API. This API serves as a single, unified interface to our microservice architecture, allowing clients to query and retrieve data from multiple services in a single request. If you''re unfamiliar with GraphQL federation, it''s an architectural pattern that combines multiple GraphQL schemas into a unified graph, enabling seamless data access across service boundaries."}]},{"type":"aside","attrs":{"variant":"info"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Want to play with our API? No problem! We have a public API available at "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://api.rawkode.academy","target":null,"rel":null,"class":null,"title":null}}],"text":"https://api.rawkode.academy"},{"type":"text","text":". You can explore the schema and run queries using the GraphQL Playground."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"We''ll cover the benefits of using a federated GraphQL API, the challenges we faced during implementation, and the tools and techniques we used to build and deploy our solution. You''ll see practical examples of how clients can interact with our API and how it dramatically simplifies the process of querying data from across multiple microservices."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"By the end of this article, you''ll understand how federated GraphQL APIs work and how they can create a more efficient and flexible API layer in a microservice architecture."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"id":"why-federated-graphql"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Why Federated GraphQL?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"In a microservice architecture, multiple services are responsible for different domains of an application. Each service has its own database and API, which can lead to clients needing to make multiple requests to different services to retrieve all necessary data."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A federated GraphQL API solves this problem by providing a single, unified interface to multiple services. Clients send a single GraphQL query to the federation layer, which handles fetching data from the appropriate services and aggregating it into a cohesive response."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"This approach offers several significant benefits:"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Reduced network requests"},{"type":"text","text":": Clients make just one request to the federated API instead of multiple requests to different services, reducing latency and bandwidth usage."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Simplified client code"},{"type":"text","text":": Developers write a single GraphQL query to retrieve data across service boundaries, eliminating complex data-fetching orchestration code."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Centralized data fetching logic"},{"type":"text","text":": The federation layer manages complex data retrieval patterns, including cross-service relationships, without burdening client applications."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Enhanced caching"},{"type":"text","text":": The federation layer can implement intelligent caching strategies, reducing load on downstream services and improving response times."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Incremental adoption"},{"type":"text","text":": Teams can add services to the federation incrementally, without disrupting existing functionality."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"id":"example"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Example"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"To understand our API and its federation, it makes sense to first see an example query."}]},{"type":"codeBlock","attrs":{"language":"graphql","title":null,"meta":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"query {\n  getLatestVideos(limit: 2) {\n    id # videos-service\n\n    title # videos-service\n\n    creditsForRole(role: \"host\") {\n      person {\n        forename # people-service\n        surname # people-service\n\n        biography  # people-biographies-service\n\n        links {\n          url  # people-links-service\n        }\n      }\n    }\n\n    likes  # video-likes-service\n  }\n}"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"As you can see from the comments above, this single query is fetching data from multiple services. The "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"getLatestVideos"},{"type":"text","text":" field is from the "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"videos-service"},{"type":"text","text":", and it''s returning the "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"id"},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"title"},{"type":"text","text":" of the video. We''re also fetching the "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"creditsForRole"},{"type":"text","text":" from the "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"people-service"},{"type":"text","text":", the "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"biography"},{"type":"text","text":" from the "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"people-biographies-service"},{"type":"text","text":", and the "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"links"},{"type":"text","text":" from the "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"people-links-service"},{"type":"text","text":". Finally, we''re fetching the "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"likes"},{"type":"text","text":" from the "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"video-likes-service"},{"type":"text","text":"."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3,"id":"adding-columns-with-a-service"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Adding Columns with a Service?!"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Yes! It''s pretty common to need to add new fields to an existing entity. Now, you could modify your micro-service and add a new column, but this requires a database migration ... which is usually OK; right? 😅"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"What if we can avoid modifying a service, writing a database migration, and potentially invalditing all presently existing backups? 🤔"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Well, we can. We can write a new service that persists the new column, all on its own and use some GraphQL magic to stitch it all together."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"This approach allows you to be more agile, take risks, and be experimental without impacting the stability of your existing services. Added a new column via a service and want to get rid of it? Easy done. Just remove the service and the column is gone."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"id":"our-federated-graphql-stack"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Our Federated GraphQL Stack"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Our federated GraphQL API is built using Apollo Federation''s specification, but we''ve chosen to implement it using a fully open-source stack."}]},{"type":"diagram","attrs":{"id":"federated-graphql-stack","title":"Federated GraphQL service stack","description":"An API client sends requests through WunderGraph Cosmo to two services. Each service uses GraphQL Yoga, Drizzle ORM, and its own libSQL or Turso database.","language":"d2","source":"API Client -> WunderGraph Cosmo\n\nWunderGraph Cosmo -> Service A\nWunderGraph Cosmo -> Service B\nService A -> GraphQL Yoga A\nGraphQL Yoga A -> Drizzle ORM A\nDrizzle ORM A -> libSQL / Turso A\n\nService B -> GraphQL Yoga B\nGraphQL Yoga B -> Drizzle ORM B\nDrizzle ORM B -> libSQL / Turso B\n","meta":"sketch","src":"https://content.rawkode.academy/articles/federated-graphql-microservices/diagrams/576d733ce064c31b.svg"}},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3,"id":"graphql-yoga"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"GraphQL Yoga"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://the-guild.dev/graphql/yoga-server","target":null,"rel":null,"class":null,"title":null}}],"text":"GraphQL Yoga"},{"type":"text","text":" serves as our GraphQL server, chosen for its:"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Flexibility"},{"type":"text","text":": Runs anywhere JavaScript can run, including Cloudflare Workers and Deno (our preferred environment over Node.js)"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Simplicity"},{"type":"text","text":": Offers straightforward setup with sensible defaults"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Extensibility"},{"type":"text","text":": Supports a rich plugin ecosystem"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Federation support"},{"type":"text","text":": Natively implements Apollo Federation specifications"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3,"id":"pothos"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Pothos"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://pothos-graphql.dev/","target":null,"rel":null,"class":null,"title":null}}],"text":"Pothos"},{"type":"text","text":" is our TypeScript schema builder, selected after evaluating several alternatives. Key advantages include:"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Type safety"},{"type":"text","text":": Provides end-to-end type safety from database to GraphQL schema"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Code-first approach"},{"type":"text","text":": Enables us to define schemas in TypeScript rather than SDL"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Plugin architecture"},{"type":"text","text":": Offers modular functionality including direct Drizzle ORM integration"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Developer experience"},{"type":"text","text":": Excellent autocompletion and type inference"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3,"id":"wundergraph-cosmo"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"WunderGraph Cosmo"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://wundergraph.com","target":null,"rel":null,"class":null,"title":null}}],"text":"WunderGraph Cosmo"},{"type":"text","text":" manages our federated graph lifecycle, offering:"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Composition checks"},{"type":"text","text":": Validates schema changes against the complete federated graph"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Router"},{"type":"text","text":": Efficiently directs queries to the appropriate subgraphs"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Analytics"},{"type":"text","text":": Provides insights into API usage patterns"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Distributed tracing"},{"type":"text","text":": Helps identify performance bottlenecks across services"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Schema registry"},{"type":"text","text":": Maintains a history of schema changes and enables rollbacks"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Caching"},{"type":"text","text":": Implements intelligent caching strategies to improve performance, at the service, row, and even column level."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"id":"building-our-federated-graphql-api"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Building Our Federated GraphQL API"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"To demonstrate the power of our approach, let''s walk through a real-world example: adding a new field to an existing entity without modifying the original service. We''ll add thumbnail capabilities to videos by creating a dedicated thumbnails service."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3,"id":"defining-the-database-schema"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Defining the Database Schema"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"We use "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://orm.drizzle.team/","target":null,"rel":null,"class":null,"title":null}}],"text":"Drizzle ORM"},{"type":"text","text":" for all our database interactions, providing type-safe queries and automated migrations."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Here''s the existing schema from our "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"videos"},{"type":"text","text":" service:"}]},{"type":"codeBlock","attrs":{"language":"typescript","title":null,"meta":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"import { createId } from ''@paralleldrive/cuid2'';\nimport { integer, sqliteTable, text } from ''drizzle-orm/sqlite-core'';\n\nexport const videosTable = sqliteTable(''videos'', {\n  id: text(''id'').primaryKey().$default(createId),\n  title: text(''title'').notNull(),\n  subtitle: text(''subtitle'').notNull(),\n  slug: text(''slug'').notNull().unique(),\n  description: text(''description'').notNull(),\n  duration: integer({ mode: ''number'' }).notNull(),\n  publishedAt: integer({ mode: ''timestamp'' }).notNull(),\n});"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now, we''ll create a new service, "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"thumbnails"},{"type":"text","text":", with its own schema:"}]},{"type":"codeBlock","attrs":{"language":"typescript","title":null,"meta":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"import { sqliteTable, text } from ''drizzle-orm/sqlite-core'';\n\nexport const thumbnailsTable = sqliteTable(''video-thumbnails'', {\n  // References the video ID from the videos service\n  id: text(''id'').notNull().unique(),\n  // URL to the thumbnail image\n  url: text(''url'').notNull(),\n});"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"This simple schema allows us to store thumbnail URLs associated with video IDs. Drizzle''s "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"drizzle-kit"},{"type":"text","text":" handles migration generation and application for us. Drizzle also has a wonderful plugin ecosystem, allowing us to convert out Drizzle types to many other formats, as well as defining DTOs for other APIs based on the database schema."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3,"id":"defining-the-graphql-schema"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Defining the GraphQL Schema"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Next, we transform our database schema into a GraphQL type using Pothos with its Drizzle plugin. The key to federation is how we reference and extend existing types. As our "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"Video"},{"type":"text","text":" type is defined in the "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"videos"},{"type":"text","text":" service, we need to reference it in our new service to be able to extend it with our new columns / fields."}]},{"type":"codeBlock","attrs":{"language":"typescript","title":null,"meta":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"import drizzlePlugin from ''@pothos/plugin-drizzle'';\nimport { eq } from ''drizzle-orm'';\nimport * as dataSchema from ''./schema.ts'';\n\n// Create a GraphQL schema builder with Drizzle plugin\nconst builder = new SchemaBuilder({\n  plugins: [drizzlePlugin()],\n});\n\n// Reference and extend the Video type from another service\nbuilder.externalRef(\n  ''Video'',\n  builder.selection<{ id: string }>(''id''),\n).implement({\n  // The ''id'' field is defined in the original videos service\n  externalFields: (t) => ({\n    id: t.string(),\n  }),\n  // Add our new thumbnail field to the Video type\n  fields: (t) => ({\n    thumbnail: t.field({\n      type: ''String'',\n      nullable: true,\n      resolve: async (video) => {\n        // Use Drizzle to query the thumbnail for this video\n        const result = await db.query.videoThumbnails.findFirst({\n          columns: {\n            url: true,\n          },\n          where: eq(dataSchema.videoThumbnails.id, video.id),\n        });\n\n        return result?.url || '''';\n      },\n    }),\n  }),\n});"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The magic of federation happens in these few lines. We''re:"}]},{"type":"orderedList","attrs":{"start":1,"type":null},"content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Declaring that we know about an external "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"Video"},{"type":"text","text":" type"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Specifying that we need the "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"id"},{"type":"text","text":" field to resolve references"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Adding our new "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"thumbnail"},{"type":"text","text":" field to the existing "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"Video"},{"type":"text","text":" type"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Providing a resolver that fetches the thumbnail URL from our database"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"This approach follows Apollo Federation''s "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://www.apollographql.com/docs/federation/entities/","target":null,"rel":null,"class":null,"title":null}}],"text":"entity resolution pattern"},{"type":"text","text":", allowing the federation layer to \"stitch together\" data from multiple services."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3,"id":"publishing-the-schema-changes"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Publishing the Schema Changes"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"After defining our schema, we need to register it with WunderGraph Cosmo. First, we generate an SDL representation of our schema:"}]},{"type":"codeBlock","attrs":{"language":"typescript","title":null,"meta":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"import { printSchemaWithDirectives } from ''@graphql-tools/utils'';\nimport { lexicographicSortSchema } from ''graphql'';\nimport { getSchema } from ''./schema.ts'';\n\n// Convert our code-first schema to SDL format\nconst schemaAsString = printSchemaWithDirectives(\n  lexicographicSortSchema(getSchema()),\n  {\n    pathToDirectivesInExtensions: [''''],\n  },\n);\n\n// Write the schema to a file\nDeno.writeFileSync(\n  `${import.meta.dirname}/schema.gql`,\n  new TextEncoder().encode(schemaAsString),\n);"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Then, we publish this schema to Cosmo using the WunderGraph CLI:"}]},{"type":"codeBlock","attrs":{"language":"bash","title":null,"meta":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"bunx wgc subgraph publish ${SERVICE_NAME} \\\n  --namespace production \\\n  --schema ./read-model/schema.gql \\\n  --routing-url https://${SERVICE_NAME}-read-458678766461.europe-west2.run.app"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Cosmo then:"}]},{"type":"orderedList","attrs":{"start":1,"type":null},"content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Validates our schema against the existing federation"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Checks for breaking changes"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Updates the router configuration"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Notifies us of any issues or successful completion"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"id":"why-we-love-this-approach"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Why We Love This Approach"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"This architecture has transformed how we build and expose our services:"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Incremental evolution"},{"type":"text","text":": We can add fields and capabilities without modifying existing services, enabling parallel development by multiple teams."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Type safety throughout"},{"type":"text","text":": With Drizzle and Pothos, we have end-to-end type safety from database to API, catching errors at compile time rather than runtime."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Developer autonomy"},{"type":"text","text":": Teams can evolve their domains independently while maintaining a cohesive API for consumers."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Operational visibility"},{"type":"text","text":": WunderGraph Cosmo provides comprehensive monitoring, alerting, and tracing, making it easier to identify and resolve issues."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Read-write separation"},{"type":"text","text":": By focusing our federation on read operations, we''ve simplified our architecture while maintaining clear command responsibility in individual services."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"id":"challenges"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Challenges"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"We''ll be honest, we''ve not hit any huge problems yet with this architecture; it''s allowed us to move fast and make mistakes without compromising the system."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"We will admit that the setup of a new service is currently a lot of copy and paste and duplicate code within our repository that could be easily generated. We''re actively exploring the "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://projen.io/","target":null,"rel":null,"class":null,"title":null}}],"text":"Projen"},{"type":"text","text":" project to help us build out a template for read-layer APIs that are generated when you run "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"bun"},{"type":"text","text":" or "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"deno"},{"type":"text","text":" install."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"This will allow us to remove the duplicated code and each read-layer service would be just the Drizzle objects and the GraphQL extension points; nice, right?"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"id":"conclusion"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Conclusion"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Federated GraphQL has proven to be a powerful solution for our microservice architecture, providing a unified API that simplifies client development while preserving service autonomy. By leveraging open-source tools like GraphQL Yoga, Pothos, and WunderGraph Cosmo, we''ve built a robust, scalable system that supports rapid development across multiple teams."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"If you''re facing challenges with API integration in a microservice environment, we highly recommend considering a federated GraphQL approach. The initial investment in setting up the federation layer pays dividends in development speed, code quality, and system performance. Thanks for reading, we promise not to leave it as long next time for the next article."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"We''ll be back with you right after KubeCon London, 2025."}]}]}' WHERE `id` = 'federated-graphql-microservices';
UPDATE `articles` SET `body` = '{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Look, I love Rust. I''m always talking about Rust. So today, we''re talking about Rust."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I''ve been building Kubernetes operators for years, mostly in Go. It''s the obvious choice: Kubernetes itself is written in Go, and "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"controller-runtime"},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"kubebuilder"},{"type":"text","text":" make scaffolding easy. But \"path of least resistance\" and \"best tool for the job\" aren''t always the same thing."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Since CoreOS introduced the operator pattern in 2016, operators have proliferated across the ecosystem, managing databases, message queues, certificates, and increasingly complex stateful workloads. As they become more critical to production infrastructure, the language they''re written in matters more than ever."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I recently joined CoreWeave, and I''m thinking a lot about how operators scale when you''re managing GPU infrastructure across hundreds of clusters. Rust''s memory efficiency and safety guarantees aren''t nice-to-haves at that scale. They''re operational necessities."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"kube-rs"},{"type":"text","text":" is a mature, production-ready Rust client for Kubernetes. It gives you everything you need to build operators with Rust''s memory safety, zero-cost abstractions, and fearless concurrency. The type system catches so many bugs that would be runtime panics in Go. If you''re building controllers that need to be reliable, efficient, and correct, it''s time to look beyond Go."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"id":"why-rust-for-kubernetes-operators"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Why Rust for Kubernetes Operators?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The obvious question: why choose Rust over Go for something so deeply embedded in the Go ecosystem?"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3,"id":"memory-safety-without-a-garbage-collector"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Memory Safety Without a Garbage Collector"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Go''s garbage collector is good, but it introduces latency spikes. For most applications, this is irrelevant. For a controller reconciling thousands of resources under pressure during a cluster incident, those pauses matter. Rust gives you deterministic memory management with zero garbage collection pauses. Your controller''s performance is predictable under load."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"More importantly, Rust''s ownership model eliminates entire classes of bugs at compile time. No null pointer dereferences. No data races. No use-after-free. When your operator is managing production databases or handling secrets rotation, these guarantees aren''t academic. They''re operational necessities."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3,"id":"type-system-as-documentation-and-guardrail"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Type System as Documentation and Guardrail"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Go''s type system is intentionally simple. Rust''s is intentionally expressive. When you''re defining Custom Resource Definitions (CRDs), Rust''s type system (with enums, pattern matching, and trait-based generics) lets you model your domain with precision that Go''s structs and interfaces can''t match."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Consider modeling the state of a managed database:"}]},{"type":"codeBlock","attrs":{"language":"rust","title":null,"meta":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"#[derive(Debug, Clone, Serialize, Deserialize, JsonSchema)]\npub enum DatabasePhase {\n    Provisioning {\n        started_at: String,\n        progress: ProvisioningStep,\n    },\n    Running {\n        endpoint: String,\n        version: String,\n        last_backup: Option<String>,\n    },\n    Upgrading {\n        from_version: String,\n        to_version: String,\n        rollback_snapshot: String,\n    },\n    Failed {\n        reason: String,\n        last_healthy_at: String,\n        recovery_attempts: u32,\n    },\n}"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"In Go, you''d represent this as a string phase field with a collection of optional fields, losing the compiler''s ability to enforce that you handle every state transition correctly. In Rust, a "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"match"},{"type":"text","text":" on "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"DatabasePhase"},{"type":"text","text":" forces you to handle every variant. You can''t forget an edge case."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3,"id":"resource-efficiency"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Resource Efficiency"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Rust binaries are compact and start fast. A typical kube-rs operator compiles to a binary often under 20 MB with release optimizations, starts in milliseconds, and uses a fraction of the memory of its Go equivalent. When you''re running operators across hundreds of clusters, this efficiency compounds into real cost savings."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"id":"getting-started-with-kube-rs"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Getting Started with kube-rs"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I''m going to walk you through building a real operator. We''ll create a controller that manages a custom "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"WebApp"},{"type":"text","text":" resource: a simplified abstraction that creates and manages a Deployment, Service, and optionally an Ingress for web applications."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3,"id":"project-setup"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Project Setup"}]},{"type":"codeBlock","attrs":{"language":"bash","title":null,"meta":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"cargo init webapp-operator\ncd webapp-operator"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Add the essential dependencies to your "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"Cargo.toml"},{"type":"text","text":":"}]},{"type":"codeBlock","attrs":{"language":"toml","title":null,"meta":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"[dependencies]\nkube = { version = \"3.0\", features = [\"runtime\", \"derive\"] }\nk8s-openapi = { version = \"0.27\", features = [\"latest\", \"schemars\"] }\nschemars = \"1\"\nserde = { version = \"1\", features = [\"derive\"] }\nserde_json = \"1\"\ntokio = { version = \"1\", features = [\"full\"] }\ntracing = \"0.1\"\ntracing-subscriber = { version = \"0.3\", features = [\"env-filter\"] }\nthiserror = \"2\"\nfutures = \"0.3\""}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3,"id":"defining-your-custom-resource"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Defining Your Custom Resource"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The CRD is where Rust''s type system really shines. kube-rs uses derive macros to generate the full CRD schema from your Rust types:"}]},{"type":"codeBlock","attrs":{"language":"rust","title":null,"meta":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"use kube::CustomResource;\nuse schemars::JsonSchema;\nuse serde::{Deserialize, Serialize};\n\n#[derive(CustomResource, Debug, Clone, Serialize, Deserialize, JsonSchema)]\n#[kube(\n    group = \"apps.rawkode.academy\",\n    version = \"v1\",\n    kind = \"WebApp\",\n    plural = \"webapps\",\n    status = \"WebAppStatus\",\n    namespaced,\n    printcolumn = r#\"{\"name\":\"Phase\",\"type\":\"string\",\"jsonPath\":\".status.phase\"}\"#,\n    printcolumn = r#\"{\"name\":\"Replicas\",\"type\":\"integer\",\"jsonPath\":\".spec.replicas\"}\"#\n)]\npub struct WebAppSpec {\n    /// Container image to deploy\n    pub image: String,\n\n    /// Number of replicas\n    #[serde(default = \"default_replicas\")]\n    pub replicas: i32,\n\n    /// Port the application listens on\n    #[serde(default = \"default_port\")]\n    pub port: i32,\n\n    /// Optional hostname for Ingress creation\n    pub hostname: Option<String>,\n\n    /// Environment variables to inject\n    #[serde(default)]\n    pub env: Vec<EnvVar>,\n\n    /// Resource requirements\n    pub resources: Option<ResourceRequirements>,\n}\n\n#[derive(Debug, Clone, Serialize, Deserialize, JsonSchema)]\npub struct EnvVar {\n    pub name: String,\n    pub value: String,\n}\n\n#[derive(Debug, Clone, Serialize, Deserialize, JsonSchema)]\npub struct ResourceRequirements {\n    pub cpu: String,\n    pub memory: String,\n}\n\n#[derive(Debug, Clone, Serialize, Deserialize, JsonSchema)]\npub struct WebAppStatus {\n    pub phase: String,\n    pub ready_replicas: i32,\n    pub message: Option<String>,\n    pub last_reconciled: Option<String>,\n}\n\nfn default_replicas() -> i32 { 3 }\nfn default_port() -> i32 { 8080 }"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"That "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"#[derive(CustomResource)]"},{"type":"text","text":" macro generates a complete "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"WebApp"},{"type":"text","text":" struct with metadata, spec, and status, plus methods to generate the CRD YAML. Call "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"WebApp::crd()"},{"type":"text","text":" and you get a fully-typed "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"CustomResourceDefinition"},{"type":"text","text":" ready to apply to your cluster."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3,"id":"the-reconciliation-loop"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The Reconciliation Loop"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The heart of any operator is its reconciliation function. kube-rs provides a "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"Controller"},{"type":"text","text":" runtime that handles watch events, requeueing, and error backoff. Your job is to implement the reconcile function:"}]},{"type":"codeBlock","attrs":{"language":"rust","title":null,"meta":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"use kube::{\n    api::{Api, Patch, PatchParams, ResourceExt},\n    runtime::controller::{Action, Controller},\n    Client, Resource,\n};\nuse std::sync::Arc;\nuse tokio::time::Duration;\n\n// Shared state for the controller\nstruct Context {\n    client: Client,\n}\n\nasync fn reconcile(\n    webapp: Arc<WebApp>,\n    ctx: Arc<Context>,\n) -> Result<Action, Error> {\n    let client = &ctx.client;\n    let name = webapp.name_any();\n    let namespace = webapp.namespace().unwrap_or_default();\n\n    tracing::info!(\n        name = %name,\n        namespace = %namespace,\n        \"Reconciling WebApp\"\n    );\n\n    // Step 1: Ensure the Deployment exists and is up-to-date\n    reconcile_deployment(client, &webapp).await?;\n\n    // Step 2: Ensure the Service exists\n    reconcile_service(client, &webapp).await?;\n\n    // Step 3: If hostname is specified, ensure Ingress exists\n    if webapp.spec.hostname.is_some() {\n        reconcile_ingress(client, &webapp).await?;\n    }\n\n    // Step 4: Update status\n    update_status(client, &webapp).await?;\n\n    // Re-reconcile every 5 minutes to catch drift\n    Ok(Action::requeue(Duration::from_secs(300)))\n}\n\nfn error_policy(\n    _webapp: Arc<WebApp>,\n    error: &Error,\n    _ctx: Arc<Context>,\n) -> Action {\n    tracing::error!(%error, \"Reconciliation failed\");\n    // Fixed retry interval of 10 seconds\n    Action::requeue(Duration::from_secs(10))\n}"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3,"id":"building-the-deployment"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Building the Deployment"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"This is where Rust''s type safety pays off. Instead of constructing untyped JSON objects, you build fully-typed Kubernetes resources:"}]},{"type":"codeBlock","attrs":{"language":"rust","title":null,"meta":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"use k8s_openapi::api::apps::v1::{Deployment, DeploymentSpec};\nuse k8s_openapi::api::core::v1::{\n    Container, ContainerPort, PodSpec, PodTemplateSpec,\n};\nuse k8s_openapi::apimachinery::pkg::apis::meta::v1::LabelSelector;\nuse std::collections::BTreeMap;\n\nasync fn reconcile_deployment(\n    client: &Client,\n    webapp: &WebApp,\n) -> Result<(), Error> {\n    let namespace = webapp.namespace().unwrap_or_default();\n    let name = webapp.name_any();\n    let deployments: Api<Deployment> =\n        Api::namespaced(client.clone(), &namespace);\n\n    let labels: BTreeMap<String, String> = BTreeMap::from([\n        (\"app.kubernetes.io/name\".into(), name.clone()),\n        (\"app.kubernetes.io/managed-by\".into(), \"webapp-operator\".into()),\n    ]);\n\n    let deployment = Deployment {\n        metadata: kube::api::ObjectMeta {\n            name: Some(name.clone()),\n            namespace: Some(namespace.clone()),\n            labels: Some(labels.clone()),\n            owner_references: Some(\n                webapp.controller_owner_ref(&()).into_iter().collect()\n            ),\n            ..Default::default()\n        },\n        spec: Some(DeploymentSpec {\n            replicas: Some(webapp.spec.replicas),\n            selector: LabelSelector {\n                match_labels: Some(labels.clone()),\n                ..Default::default()\n            },\n            template: PodTemplateSpec {\n                metadata: Some(kube::api::ObjectMeta {\n                    labels: Some(labels),\n                    ..Default::default()\n                }),\n                spec: Some(PodSpec {\n                    containers: vec![Container {\n                        name: \"app\".into(),\n                        image: Some(webapp.spec.image.clone()),\n                        ports: Some(vec![ContainerPort {\n                            container_port: webapp.spec.port,\n                            ..Default::default()\n                        }]),\n                        ..Default::default()\n                    }],\n                    ..Default::default()\n                }),\n            },\n            ..Default::default()\n        }),\n        ..Default::default()\n    };\n\n    deployments\n        .patch(\n            &name,\n            &PatchParams::apply(\"webapp-operator\"),\n            &Patch::Apply(deployment),\n        )\n        .await?;\n\n    Ok(())\n}"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Notice the "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"owner_references"},{"type":"text","text":" line. This sets up Kubernetes garbage collection so that when a "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"WebApp"},{"type":"text","text":" is deleted, its Deployment is automatically cleaned up. kube-rs provides "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"controller_owner_ref"},{"type":"text","text":" as a convenience method that returns an "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"Option<OwnerReference>"},{"type":"text","text":". The "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":".into_iter().collect()"},{"type":"text","text":" pattern cleanly converts it into the "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"Vec"},{"type":"text","text":" that "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"owner_references"},{"type":"text","text":" expects."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"id":"architecture-how-it-all-fits-together"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Architecture: How It All Fits Together"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Here''s how the operator interacts with the Kubernetes API and manages its resources:"}]},{"type":"diagram","attrs":{"id":null,"title":null,"description":null,"language":"d2","source":"direction: right\n\nuser: User {\n  shape: person\n}\n\napiserver: Kubernetes API Server {\n  style.fill: \"#326CE5\"\n  style.font-color: \"#ffffff\"\n}\n\noperator: WebApp Operator {\n  style.fill: \"#CE412B\"\n  style.font-color: \"#ffffff\"\n\n  watcher: Watch Stream\n  reconciler: Reconcile Loop\n  queue: Work Queue\n\n  watcher -> queue: enqueue events\n  queue -> reconciler: dequeue\n}\n\nwebapp_crd: WebApp CR {\n  style.fill: \"#DEA584\"\n}\n\ndeployment: Deployment {\n  style.fill: \"#4A9BD9\"\n}\n\nservice: Service {\n  style.fill: \"#4A9BD9\"\n}\n\ningress: Ingress {\n  style.fill: \"#4A9BD9\"\n}\n\nuser -> apiserver: kubectl apply WebApp\napiserver -> webapp_crd: stores\napiserver -> operator.watcher: watch events\noperator.reconciler -> apiserver: create/update resources\napiserver -> deployment: stores\napiserver -> service: stores\napiserver -> ingress: stores (if hostname set)","meta":"sketch","src":"https://content.rawkode.academy/articles/kubernetes-operators-rust-kube-rs/diagrams/2000ba267a04c76f.svg"}},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The operator watches for changes to "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"WebApp"},{"type":"text","text":" custom resources. When a change is detected, the watcher enqueues a reconciliation event. The reconciler then ensures the actual state (Deployments, Services, Ingresses) matches the desired state defined in the "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"WebApp"},{"type":"text","text":" spec. This is the standard level-triggered reconciliation pattern, the same one that powers every built-in Kubernetes controller."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"id":"testing-your-operator"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Testing Your Operator"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"One of kube-rs''s underrated strengths is its testing story. The library provides utilities for both unit testing with mocked API responses and integration testing against real clusters."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3,"id":"unit-testing-with-mocked-responses"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Unit Testing with Mocked Responses"}]},{"type":"codeBlock","attrs":{"language":"rust","title":null,"meta":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"#[cfg(test)]\nmod tests {\n    use super::*;\n    use kube::api::ObjectMeta;\n\n    fn test_webapp() -> WebApp {\n        WebApp {\n            metadata: ObjectMeta {\n                name: Some(\"test-app\".into()),\n                namespace: Some(\"default\".into()),\n                ..Default::default()\n            },\n            spec: WebAppSpec {\n                image: \"nginx:latest\".into(),\n                replicas: 2,\n                port: 80,\n                hostname: Some(\"test.example.com\".into()),\n                env: vec![],\n                resources: None,\n            },\n            status: None,\n        }\n    }\n\n    #[test]\n    fn test_crd_generation() {\n        // Verify CRD schema is valid\n        let crd = WebApp::crd();\n        assert_eq!(crd.metadata.name, Some(\"webapps.apps.rawkode.academy\".into()));\n    }\n\n    #[test]\n    fn test_default_values() {\n        let spec: WebAppSpec = serde_json::from_str(r#\"{\n            \"image\": \"nginx:latest\"\n        }\"#).unwrap();\n        assert_eq!(spec.replicas, 3);\n        assert_eq!(spec.port, 8080);\n    }\n}"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3,"id":"integration-testing"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Integration Testing"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"For integration tests, kube-rs works well with tools like "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"k3d"},{"type":"text","text":" or "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"kind"},{"type":"text","text":". Spin up an ephemeral cluster in CI:"}]},{"type":"codeBlock","attrs":{"language":"rust","title":null,"meta":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"#[tokio::test]\n#[ignore = \"requires cluster\"]\nasync fn test_full_reconciliation() {\n    let client = Client::try_default().await.unwrap();\n    let webapps: Api<WebApp> = Api::namespaced(client.clone(), \"default\");\n\n    // Create a WebApp\n    let webapp = WebApp::new(\"integration-test\", WebAppSpec {\n        image: \"nginx:latest\".into(),\n        replicas: 1,\n        port: 80,\n        hostname: None,\n        env: vec![],\n        resources: None,\n    });\n\n    webapps.create(&Default::default(), &webapp).await.unwrap();\n\n    // Wait for reconciliation\n    tokio::time::sleep(Duration::from_secs(5)).await;\n\n    // Verify Deployment was created\n    let deployments: Api<Deployment> =\n        Api::namespaced(client.clone(), \"default\");\n    let deploy = deployments.get(\"integration-test\").await.unwrap();\n    assert_eq!(\n        deploy.spec.unwrap().replicas,\n        Some(1)\n    );\n}"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"id":"the-main-function-wiring-it-all-together"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The Main Function: Wiring It All Together"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Here''s the complete entrypoint that starts the controller:"}]},{"type":"codeBlock","attrs":{"language":"rust","title":null,"meta":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"use futures::StreamExt;\n\n#[tokio::main]\nasync fn main() -> Result<(), Box<dyn std::error::Error>> {\n    tracing_subscriber::fmt()\n        .with_env_filter(\"webapp_operator=info,kube=info\")\n        .json()\n        .init();\n\n    let client = Client::try_default().await?;\n\n    // Ensure CRD exists (or apply it separately)\n    let webapps: Api<WebApp> = Api::all(client.clone());\n    let deployments: Api<Deployment> = Api::all(client.clone());\n\n    tracing::info!(\"Starting WebApp operator\");\n\n    Controller::new(webapps, Default::default())\n        // Watch owned Deployments for changes\n        .owns(deployments, Default::default())\n        .run(reconcile, error_policy, Arc::new(Context { client }))\n        .for_each(|result| async move {\n            match result {\n                Ok(obj) => tracing::info!(resource = ?obj, \"Reconciled\"),\n                Err(err) => tracing::error!(%err, \"Reconciliation error\"),\n            }\n        })\n        .await;\n\n    Ok(())\n}"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":".owns(deployments, Default::default())"},{"type":"text","text":" call is crucial. It tells the controller to also watch Deployments and trigger reconciliation of the owning "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"WebApp"},{"type":"text","text":" if a managed Deployment changes. This is how your operator detects drift: if someone manually edits a Deployment, the controller immediately reconciles it back to the desired state."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"id":"containerizing-and-deploying"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Containerizing and Deploying"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Rust''s compilation model makes containerization straightforward. Here''s a multi-stage Dockerfile:"}]},{"type":"codeBlock","attrs":{"language":"dockerfile","title":null,"meta":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"FROM rust:1.93 AS builder\nWORKDIR /build\nCOPY . .\nRUN cargo build --release\n\nFROM gcr.io/distroless/cc-debian12:nonroot\nCOPY --from=builder /build/target/release/webapp-operator /\nENTRYPOINT [\"/webapp-operator\"]"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The final image is typically under 30 MB. Compare this to Go operator images, which with similar distroless builds land around 30-50 MB, or larger when you start including debugging tools. Java-based operators (yes, they exist) can exceed 300 MB."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"id":"when-to-choose-rust-over-go"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"When to Choose Rust Over Go"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I want to be straight with you. Rust isn''t always the right choice for every operator."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Choose Rust when:"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Your operator manages security-sensitive resources (secrets, certificates, network policies)"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You need predictable, low-latency reconciliation under high load"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Your team already has Rust experience"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You''re building a long-lived project where compile-time correctness pays dividends"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Resource efficiency matters (edge deployments, large-scale multi-cluster)"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Stick with Go when:"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You''re prototyping and need maximum iteration speed"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Your team is Go-native and switching would slow delivery"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You need deep integration with controller-runtime''s ecosystem (e.g., Kubebuilder scaffolding)"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The operator is simple enough that Go''s simplicity is an advantage"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The ecosystem around kube-rs is growing steadily. "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"kopium"},{"type":"text","text":" generates Rust types from existing CRDs, and the broader kube-rs project (a CNCF Sandbox project) continues to add tooling that reduces boilerplate. The community is smaller than Go''s operator ecosystem, but it''s active, welcoming, and shipping real production software."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"id":"production-readiness-checklist"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Production Readiness Checklist"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Before shipping your Rust operator to production, make sure you''ve covered these essentials:"}]},{"type":"taskList","content":[{"type":"taskItem","attrs":{"checked":false},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"RBAC manifests"},{"type":"text","text":": Generate ClusterRole/ClusterRoleBinding with minimum required permissions"}]}]},{"type":"taskItem","attrs":{"checked":false},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Leader election"},{"type":"text","text":": Use a leader election crate such as "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"kube-leader-election"},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"kube-coordinate"},{"type":"text","text":", or "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"kube-lease-manager"},{"type":"text","text":" to prevent split-brain in HA deployments"}]}]},{"type":"taskItem","attrs":{"checked":false},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Metrics endpoint"},{"type":"text","text":": Expose Prometheus metrics for reconciliation duration, queue depth, and errors"}]}]},{"type":"taskItem","attrs":{"checked":false},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Health probes"},{"type":"text","text":": Implement "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"/healthz"},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"/readyz"},{"type":"text","text":" endpoints for Kubernetes liveness and readiness checks"}]}]},{"type":"taskItem","attrs":{"checked":false},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Graceful shutdown"},{"type":"text","text":": Handle SIGTERM to complete in-flight reconciliations before exiting"}]}]},{"type":"taskItem","attrs":{"checked":false},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Finalizers"},{"type":"text","text":": Add finalizers to handle custom cleanup logic when resources are deleted"}]}]},{"type":"taskItem","attrs":{"checked":false},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Status subresource"},{"type":"text","text":": Always write meaningful status conditions so users can "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"kubectl get webapps"},{"type":"text","text":" and understand state"}]}]},{"type":"taskItem","attrs":{"checked":false},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Owner references"},{"type":"text","text":": Set owner references on all created resources for automatic garbage collection"}]}]},{"type":"taskItem","attrs":{"checked":false},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Event recording"},{"type":"text","text":": Emit Kubernetes Events so "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"kubectl describe"},{"type":"text","text":" shows what the operator is doing"}]}]},{"type":"taskItem","attrs":{"checked":false},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"E2E tests in CI"},{"type":"text","text":": Run integration tests against an ephemeral cluster (k3d/kind) on every PR"}]}]},{"type":"taskItem","attrs":{"checked":false},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"CRD versioning strategy"},{"type":"text","text":": Plan for how you''ll handle CRD schema evolution (conversion webhooks if needed)"}]}]},{"type":"taskItem","attrs":{"checked":false},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Container image signing"},{"type":"text","text":": Sign images with cosign and publish SBOMs for supply chain security"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"id":"the-bigger-picture"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The Bigger Picture"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"We''re past the point where \"it works\" is good enough for operators. They need to be secure, efficient, and maintainable over years of production use. Rust''s guarantees align perfectly with those requirements."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"kube-rs has proven itself in production at scale. It''s maintained by an active community, has excellent documentation, and the tooling ecosystem keeps growing. If you''ve been curious about Rust but haven''t found the right project to start with, a Kubernetes operator is a great place to begin. You get practical Rust experience while building something genuinely useful for your infrastructure."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The cloud native world doesn''t have to be a Go monoculture. Diversity in implementation languages leads to diversity in ideas and approaches. Your next operator might just be your first Rust project."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Give it a try. Your future on-call self will thank you."}]}]}' WHERE `id` = 'kubernetes-operators-rust-kube-rs';
